< [2026-10-28T07:50:10Z] checkpoint of #3 (a step) pushed to looper/gateway-3:  2 files changed, 256 insertions(+)
I stopped issue #3 without committing. I posted the open questions and my recommendations on the issue ([comment](https://github.com/tacplus-dev/gateway/issues/3#issuecomment-6463629018)) and added the `needs-decision` label. Nothing changed on main, no gates ran, and the issue stays open.

Three things outside this repository have to happen first, and none has an issue yet:

1. **There is no compiler that can build the routing yet.** tacplus#170 is on tacplus main but unreleased; the newest release is v2.4.1, and 2.4 has no route fields. I tried the published build of tacplus main, which includes #170. It refuses every repository's declared compiler version, including kernel's, so nothing in the fleet can build against it. I recommended releasing tacplus v2.5.0 now, or a properly versioned pre-release if 2.5 has to wait for tacplus#163.
2. **tenantecho and doppelganger don't declare route fields.** The gateway's tests depend on both. Under 2.5 the compiler refuses to build a tenant-owned service whose calls don't name their target tenant, so the gateway's build would fail. I recommended one issue on each repository and asked for approval before filing them, since they change another repository's contract.
3. **Tenant instances aren't registered by tenant id.** The gateway finds a tenant's instance by a name built from its certificate identity, and that identity still uses the tenant's name (kernel#3 left that out of scope). The new route field carries the 8-character tenant id. I recommended giving tenant instances identities based on the id, so the gateway stays a single lookup. That touches kernel, the operator and tacplus. The alternative is the gateway looking up id-to-name in kernel on each call.

The comment also lays out the gateway change for when these are settled:
- One function decides the target for both the REST and gRPC paths, so a placement resolver can slot in there later.
- Streams are routed by their first message, and every later message must name the same tenant.
- Routing from the caller's token is removed.
- The end-to-end routing test is rewritten to the issue's acceptance criteria.
- The MCP follow-up, #15, is folded into the same change.

The run is checkpointed to `looper/gateway-3`.
